title: Atomic Environments
keyword: ENVIRON
manual: structure/#c2-environ
cite: econ_1979 econ_2016

Shows the environment of one atom of the system in the "System" line: which neighbors are
coordinated to it, its coordination numbers and effective coordination numbers (ECoN) per
species, its coordination polyhedron, its radial distribution, and the list of its neighbors
with their distances and ECoN weights. It brings together, for one atom at a time, what the
ENVIRON, ECON, COORD, POLYHEDRA and RDF keywords calculate for all of them. The atom and its
coordinated neighbors are marked in the view while the window is open, and everything is
recalculated whenever the atom, the options, the bonds or the geometry change. During a
dynamics run the window keeps the last values, and updates when the run stops.

# Options

- "Center": the atom whose environment is shown, in its color in the view: its species, its
  index in the unit cell and, if it is not in the main cell, the lattice vector of its image.
  "Pick" lets you choose it by clicking it in the view; in a crystal, this also chooses which
  image of the atom is marked. A new system starts with its first atom.
- "Coordinated": which neighbors are coordinated to the atom. "Bonded": the atoms bonded to it
  in the bond list of the system (which the bonds objects draw unless they use their own
  criteria; it includes the bonds added or removed by hand). "Covalent radii": the atoms closer
  than the bond factor times the sum of the covalent radii, with the radii and bond factor of
  the system (those used to calculate its bonds), as COORD does. "ECoN weight": the atoms whose
  weight in the ECoN is at least 0.5, as ECON counts its integer coordination number.
- "Up to": list the neighbors up to a distance from the atom (in Å), or up to a number of
  shells. A shell is the group of neighbors at the same distance, which may be of different
  kinds.
- "Species": list only the neighbors of one species.
- "Group by shells": one row per shell and atom (the atoms of a shell that are images of the
  same non-equivalent atom), with their number ("Count") and how many of them are
  coordinated.
- "Show in view": mark the atom (orange halo) and its coordinated neighbors (blue halos) in the
  view. The neighbors of the row under the mouse are marked in yellow, with a line from the
  atom.

# Coordination

The table has a row for all species and one for each species in the environment of the atom:
the coordination number ("CN", the coordinated neighbors of that species), the ECoN (the
iterative variant, and the non-iterative one, which starts from the shortest distance), the
distance to the nearest atom of that species, the mean weighted distance of the ECoN ("Mean"),
and the geometry of the coordinated neighbors. The ECoN weight of an atom at distance d is
exp(1 - (d/d_av)^6), where d_av is the mean weighted distance (Hoppe's formula, iterated as in
ECON).

"Geometry" is the closest ideal shape with the same number of vertices (linear, trigonal
planar, tetrahedral, square planar, octahedral, trigonal prismatic, cuboctahedral, and so on),
of all the coordinated neighbors in the first row and of those of one species in the others.
The angles between the neighbors at the atom are sorted and compared with those of the shape;
the number in parentheses is the root mean square difference, in degrees, zero for a perfect
match. Only the angles count, not the distances, so a Jahn-Teller octahedron is a perfect
octahedron. The tooltip gives the next closest shape: two close numbers mean the environment
is halfway between them. There are shapes for 2 to 10, 12 and 14 neighbors. The ideal square
pyramid has the atom above the base (100° from the apex to the base atoms), so an octahedron
with a missing vertex is a square pyramid 11° off.

"Polyhedron" gives the coordination polyhedron, the convex hull of the coordinated neighbors:
its numbers of vertices and faces, its volume, the shortest and longest distance from the atom
to the vertices, and its planarity, the largest distance from a vertex to the plane that best
fits all of them (zero for a flat polyhedron). A polyhedron needs at least four vertices.

# Radial distribution

The plot shows the neighbors of the atom as a function of the distance, each broadened into a
gaussian of unit area whose standard deviation is "Width (Å)", so the area under the curve
between two distances is the number of neighbors between them ("Neighbors per Å", left axis).
The staircase is the running coordination number, the number of neighbors closer than each
distance (right axis). Both count the species chosen in "Species" of the Neighbors section,
and the plot goes as far as the list (in shells mode, four widths beyond the last shell).
Hover the plot to mark in the view, in yellow, the neighbors within two widths of the distance
under the mouse; the line below the plot gives their number and the coordination number at
that distance. Unlike the RDF keyword, this is the distribution around one atom, not normalized
by the density or by 4πr². The section is closed when the window opens.

# Neighbors

The table has one row per neighbor, sorted by distance: its shell, name, index in the list of
non-equivalent atoms ("Id") and in the unit cell ("Cell id"), the lattice vector of its image
next to the marked atom (crystals), the distance, whether it is coordinated, and its ECoN
weight (with the mean weighted distance of all species). Right-click the header of either
table to copy it or write it to a file.
